  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nFollowing a chilly start to the day, sunny skies are boosting\ntemperatures back through the 50s early this afternoon.\nMeanwhile, light winds (~5 mph or less) to nearly calm\nconditions persist as the center of surface high pressure slides\neastward into the Mid-Mississippi Valley. This light wind regime\nwill continue tonight under clear skies as the high shifts into\nthe Ohio Valley. Consequently, another chilly night is expected\nacross the area, particularly away from the Chicago urban heat \nisland. Overnight lows are projected to bottom out in the low to\nmid-30s (40s in the city), with frost formation possible once \nagain. \n\nNo changes were made to the ongoing Frost Advisory in effect \nfor areas generally east of I-55, as this region remains the \nmost favored for continued nocturnal decoupling with little to \nno surface wind through daybreak Sunday morning. Conversely, \nareas north of the advisory area may see light southerly winds \ndevelop prior to daybreak as the center of the surface high \nshifts further east. Accordingly, while temperatures will be \ncomparable, subtle boundary layer mixing may be sufficient to \nlimit widespread frost formation outside the current advisory \narea. \n\nFollowing another cool morning, pleasant weather is expected \nacross the region Sunday, though southwesterly winds will turn \nslightly breezy by the afternoon. Afternoon highs should rebound\ninto the low-to-mid 60s under mostly sunny skies. Thereafter, \ndry and unseasonably mild weather will persist through the \nupcoming week as high pressure dominates the Great Lakes. \nTemperatures will trend upward into the mid-to-upper 60s on \nMonday, peaking in the low-to-mid 70s (warmest inland) from \nTuesday onward. While remaining below record thresholds (record \nhighs in the lower 80s at Chicago and Rockford), these \ntemperatures are well above the seasonal average high of ~60 \ndegrees.\n\nKJB",
